---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PlanCard from '../components/PlanCard.astro';
import { narrativeImage, itemImage } from '../lib/images';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';

const leadImage = await narrativeImage('fig02-mews-entrance-1964.jpg');

// The two Municipal Archives sheets, each scanned front and back.
const [planAFront, planABack, planBFront, planBBack] = await Promise.all([
  itemImage('folder12a.jpg'),
  itemImage('folder12a-back.jpg'),
  itemImage('folder12b.jpg'),
  itemImage('folder12b-back.jpg'),
]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Space"
  description="The Greek Revival church at 141–145 West 13th Street, the interfaith covenant between its Presbyterian and Jewish congregations, and the basement auditorium that became the Greenwich Mews Theater."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero__inner">
      <h1 class="page-hero__title fade-in">The Space</h1>
      <p class="page-hero__subtitle fade-in">One House Serving Two Faiths</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="lead-figure section">
    <div class="container">
      <figure class="lead-figure__figure fade-in">
        <div class="img-frame">
          <Image
            src={leadImage}
            alt="The Village Presbyterian Church portico in 1964. A hanging sign reads Greenwich Mews Theatre Entrance, and a banner below it advertises the production of Jerico-Jim Crow."
            widths={[600, 1000, 1600]}
            sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px"
            loading="eager"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="lead-figure__caption">
          The Greenwich Mews Theatre entrance on West 13th Street, c.&nbsp;1964, with a banner for
          Langston Hughes's <em>Jerico-Jim Crow</em>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="venue section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h2 class="section-heading fade-in">The Building</h2>

      <div class="venue__content fade-in">
        <p>
          Architect Samuel Thomson designed the Greek Revival church building at 141–145 West
          13th Street, erected between 1839 and 1846. Fires in 1855 and 1902 necessitated major
          reconstructions, always preserving its original building design. After a series of
          mergers between downtown Presbyterian churches, the church was renamed the Village
          Presbyterian in the 1950s.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="venue venue--alt section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h2 class="section-heading fade-in">A Covenant of Brotherhood</h2>

      <div class="venue__content fade-in">
        <p>
          In 1948, Rabbi Julian F. Fleg proposed to Rev. Jesse Stitt that his newly opened
          Village Temple rent a room in the church's recreation building for their Friday night
          services. The pastor offered Fleg the use of the main sanctuary of the church instead.
          In 1952, the arrival of a young rabbi, Irving Block, launched the next phase of the
          interfaith collaboration when his Brotherhood Synagogue replaced the Village Temple as
          co-equal residents of the church premises and codified the arrangement with a "Covenant
          of Brotherhood" between the pastor and rabbi.
        </p>
        <p>
          A sign on the gate outside the main sanctuary read "One House Serving Two Faiths."
          Press coverage celebrated the ecumenical arrangement as the only church-temple of its
          kind in the nation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="venue section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h2 class="section-heading fade-in">The Basement Auditorium</h2>

      <div class="venue__content fade-in">
        <p>
          The auditorium in its basement also transformed across the decades. In the 1940s Erwin
          Piscator's nearby Dramatic Workshop utilized the space; the pastor's wife operated an
          amateur drama group there, as did the Greenwich Mews Players, whose director wanted to
          provide theatrical opportunities for returning servicemen and women. The Equity Library
          Theatre (ELT), a pioneering training ground for aspiring actors—and one of the few that
          cast Black performers in robust dramatic roles at the time—remounted its most popular
          offerings there, under the name the Greenwich Mews Library Theatre. An experimental
          collective, Lemonade Opera, also called the space its home beginning in 1947.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="plans venue--alt section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-heading fade-in">The Plans</h2>

      <p class="plans__lede fade-in">
        Two sheets survive in the Municipal Archives, filed with the Department of Buildings in
        1946 — a seating diagram for the basement auditorium, drawn in pencil and printed as a
        cyanotype. Turn either sheet over for the approval stamps on its back.
      </p>

      <div class="plans__grid fade-in">
        <PlanCard
          id="plan-blueprint"
          title="The cyanotype print"
          front={planAFront}
          back={planABack}
          frontAlt="Cyanotype blueprint of the seating diagram for the basement auditorium, white lines on deep blue, showing rows of seats, the stage platform, dressing rooms and a choir room."
          backAlt="The back of the blueprint sheet: blank paper carrying a Department of Housing and Buildings approval stamp dated November 1946 and pencilled block and lot numbers."
          caption="Seating diagram of the basement auditorium, printed as a blueprint. The rows of seats, the platform, the dressing rooms and the outer courts on West 13th Street are all laid out."
          backCaption="The verso: a Borough of Manhattan approval stamp dated 15 November 1946, and the block and lot renumbered by hand to 609/65."
        />
        <PlanCard
          id="plan-drawing"
          title="The architect's drawing"
          front={planBFront}
          back={planBBack}
          frontAlt="The original pencil drawing on vellum of the same seating diagram, with a section drawing above it, a plot plan to the right, and a title block giving the seat count."
          backAlt="The back of the vellum sheet, carrying approval stamps and signatures that read in mirror image through the translucent paper."
          caption="The original drawing on vellum, carrying far more than the print: a section through the room, a plot plan of the lot, and a title block recording 216 fixed seats and 24 for personnel — 240 in all."
          backCaption="The verso, stamped and signed. The sheet is translucent, so the marks read in mirror image through the paper."
        />
      </div>

      <p class="plans__credit fade-in">
        Map of Basement, 145 W. 13th Street, Ralph H. Segal, Architect, March 22, 1946–April 10,
        1946. Department of Buildings architectural drawings and plans of Lower Manhattan, circa
        1866–1978; REC 0074; Municipal Archives, City of New York.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="venue section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h2 class="section-heading fade-in">1951: The Greenwich Mews</h2>

      <div class="venue__content fade-in">
        <p>
          In 1951, an interracial collective of actors and directors formed a new membership
          theater, The Greenwich Mews, in the basement auditorium. Among the founders were
          administrative coordinator Stella Holt; producer Lily Turner; actor, director, and
          teacher Osceola Archer; and actors R. Graham Browne and Salem Ludwig. Their mission
          statement echoed the neighborly imperatives of their co-sponsors, pledging to operate
          "without preference for or prejudice to race, creed, or color."
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="venue venue--alt section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h2 class="section-heading fade-in">Afterward</h2>

      <div class="venue__content fade-in">
        <p>
          The Brotherhood Synagogue left the church building in 1974; the church dissolved in
          1975. In 1982, architect Stephen B. Jacobs converted the space into an 11-unit
          residential coop, preserving its original façade.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="venue-links section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="section-heading section-heading--center fade-in">Explore Further</h2>

      <div class="venue-links__grid fade-in stagger">
        <a href={withBase('/archive')} class="venue-link">
          <h4>Archive</h4>
          <p>Photographs, playbills, and documents from the building and the theater</p>
        </a>
        <a href={withBase('/people')} class="venue-link">
          <h4>People &amp; Documents</h4>
          <p>The founders, clergy, and artists who made the Mews</p>
        </a>
        <a href={withBase('/productions')} class="venue-link">
          <h4>Productions</h4>
          <p>Every production staged in the basement auditorium, 1951–1973</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Page Hero */
  .page-hero {
    padding: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-md) var(--space-xl);
    text-align: center;
    background: var(--color-parchment);
    position: relative;
  }

  .page-hero::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    height: 1px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, var(--color-sepia), transparent);
  }

  .page-hero__inner {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .page-hero__title {
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .page-hero__subtitle {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-tobacco);
  }

  /* Section Headings */
  .section-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.15em;
    color: var(--color-tobacco);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    position: relative;
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .section-heading::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    bottom: -0.5em;
    left: 0;
    width: 40px;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--color-rust);
  }

  .section-heading--center {
    display: block;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .section-heading--center::after {
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }

  /* Lead figure */
  .lead-figure__figure {
    margin: 0 auto;
    max-width: 900px;
  }

  .lead-figure__figure .img-frame {
    aspect-ratio: 1918 / 1300;
  }

  .lead-figure__caption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-tobacco);
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  /* Plans */
  .plans__lede {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .plans__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .plans__credit {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-tobacco);
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
    padding-top: var(--space-sm);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-sepia);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .plans__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  /* Venue prose */
  .venue--alt {
    background: var(--color-parchment);
  }

  .venue__content p {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .venue__content p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* Explore Further */
  .venue-links {
    background: var(--color-parchment);
  }

  .venue-links__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-md);
  }

  .venue-link {
    display: block;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-sepia);
    background: var(--color-cream);
    transition: border-color var(--transition-fast), transform var(--transition-fast);
  }

  .venue-link:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-rust);
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }

  .venue-link h4 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 0.25em;
  }

  .venue-link p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-tobacco);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .venue-links__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .section-heading {
      display: block;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .section-heading::after {
      left: 50%;
      transform: translateX(-50%);
    }
  }
</style>
